   <text:h text:style-name="P6" text:outline-level="1" text:is-list-header="true">Scriptoria Builds Provide URLs</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P7">During a build of an Android App being published to Google Play, if the project is using the <text:span text:style-name="T2">User Accounts </text:span>feature and the <text:span text:style-name="T6">Delete Account URL</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5"> field</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="T5">is</text:span> blank, then Scriptoria will populate <text:span text:style-name="T5">both of </text:span>the URLs with links to pages in Scriptoria to allow the end user to <text:span text:style-name="T5">submit</text:span> data deletion requests. <text:span text:style-name="T7">These URLs will be included in the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">Data Safety CSV export</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> created during the build.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P8" text:outline-level="1">Scriptoria Data Management Form</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P7"><text:span text:style-name="T7">The Scriptoria user data management page contains a</text:span> form <text:span text:style-name="T7">that </text:span>prompts the user for their email address <text:span text:style-name="T7">and</text:span> the type of deletion, either “Delete my data” or “Delete my account and all associated data”. It also describes the items <text:span text:style-name="T7">that will</text:span> removed. It uses a verification code sent to the email address specified to <text:span text:style-name="T7">authenticate</text:span> the person requesting the <text:span text:style-name="T7">page.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P9" />
   <text:p text:style-name="P7"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr2" draw:name="Image1" text:anchor-type="char" svg:x="0.4846in" svg:y="0.1791in" svg:width="5.3154in" svg:height="9.0362in" draw:z-index="0"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
